// examples/quant/implied_vol — deterministic implied volatility by a bracketed
// root-find with a FIXED iteration count.
//
// Copyright 2025 STARGA Inc.
// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
// Part of the MIND project (Machine Intelligence Native Design).
//
// WHY THIS MODEL EARNS ITS PLACE IN THE SET.  `examples/quant/black_scholes`
// prices an option in closed form; `examples/quant/crr_binomial` prices one by
// a fixed lattice.  Both are straight-line computations: the same arithmetic
// happens on every input, and the only thing a hostile substrate can perturb is
// the last bit of a result.  Implied volatility is categorically different, and
// it is where determinism bites hardest of anything in this set.
//
// Implied vol is an ITERATIVE ROOT-FIND.  It has no closed form.  The answer is
// not a function of the inputs alone — it is a function of the exact SEQUENCE
// OF FLOATING-POINT COMPARISONS the iteration took to get there.  Consider what
// a single flipped comparison does:
//
//   * The sign test `f > 0` decides which half of the bracket survives.  If it
//     flips at the last bit on one substrate, the two substrates keep DIFFERENT
//     brackets from that step onward.
//   * Every subsequent step is computed from that bracket, so the divergence
//     does not stay at the last bit.  It compounds: the next Newton step is
//     taken from a different point, lands somewhere else, and is accepted or
//     rejected by a different in-bracket test.
//   * The two runs then return different roots.  Not "different in the last
//     bit" — different by however much the trajectories separated.
//
// The usual formulation makes this strictly worse by also making the TRIP COUNT
// data-dependent:
//
//     while absf(f) > tol { ... }      // <-- the bug this example refutes
//
// That loop runs a number of times decided by a floating-point comparison.  On
// a substrate where `absf(f)` lands one ulp on the other side of `tol`, the
// loop runs one more time — or one fewer — and returns a different number.  The
// divergence is invisible in the closed-form price (there is none to inspect)
// and it is impossible to bound after the fact, because "how many iterations
// did the other machine take" is not recoverable from the answer.
//
// THE FIX, AND WHY IT IS THE HONEST ONE.  This module runs a FIXED, statically
// known 60 iterations, always, on every input, with no early exit and no
// convergence test.  Both arms of every branch do the same amount of work.  The
// trip count is therefore a compile-time constant and cannot be a function of
// any datum.  What remains variable is only WHICH branch each step takes, and
// the bracket invariant is what makes that safe: the interval only ever
// shrinks, the root is inside it at every step, and after a fixed number of
// guaranteed halvings the interval is narrower than the answer's precision.  A
// substrate that takes a different branch still converges to the same bracket.
//
// This costs iterations that a convergence test would have saved.  That trade —
// a bounded amount of wasted arithmetic in exchange for a trip count no input
// can influence — is the whole point.  Paying for determinism in cycles is the
// cheapest currency available; paying for it in "the other machine got a
// different vol" is not a price anyone can settle.
//
// WHY NEWTON AT ALL, IF BISECTION ALONE IS SAFE.  Bisection alone converges
// linearly: 60 halvings of [1e-4, 5] reaches about 4.3e-18, which is adequate
// but leaves the answer's last bits set by the bracket midpoint rather than by
// the function.  The hybrid takes a Newton step when — and only when — that
// step lands STRICTLY INSIDE the current bracket, and bisects otherwise.  The
// bracket is updated first, from the sign test, so the Newton candidate is
// always vetted against an interval that already contains the root.  A Newton
// step that overshoots is silently discarded; it can never move the iterate
// outside the bracket, so it can never break the convergence guarantee.  The
// acceptance test is itself a comparison, so it is part of the branch sequence
// described above — and that is exactly why the bracket, not the test, is what
// carries the correctness argument.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT A LIBRARY.  Inverting Black-Scholes for sigma is standard
// practice and nothing here is a contribution to finance.  Brent's method would
// converge faster; a Jaeckel-style rational initial guess would converge in
// three steps rather than sixty.  Neither is used, because both buy speed with
// input-dependent control flow, and this example exists to demonstrate the
// absence of exactly that.  The contribution is that every transcendental on
// the path — exp, log, sqrt, erfc — is rebuilt from `+ - * /` on the strict
// scalar path in `std.detmath`, and the control flow above them is bounded by
// construction, so the recovered vol is reproducible bit-for-bit on a different
// ISA rather than merely close.
//
// THE CLAIM, STATED EXACTLY.  This module does NOT claim to match any host
// libm, SciPy, or a vendor analytics library in the last bits, and it does not
// claim to recover the exact sigma that produced a price.  It cannot: the
// forward price is a rounded f64, many sigmas map to it, and the inverse can
// only land in that preimage.  The claim is narrower and harder:
//     the same source, compiled for two different ISAs, performs the same fixed
//     number of iterations and produces byte-identical output, with a stated
//     accuracy bound against a 50-digit oracle.
// Accuracy and reproducibility are orthogonal properties.  Determinism is what
// makes an error analysis meaningful, not a substitute for one.
//
// WHAT THE BANDS BELOW ARE REALLY MEASURING.  An implied-vol error band is not
// a pricing error band.  A perturbation of the input price is amplified into
// the recovered vol by 1/vega, and vega is small exactly where the option is
// deep in or out of the money.  Measured on these cases, one ulp of price error
// becomes up to 78 ulps of vol error at S=150 (vega = 3.55) and 13 ulps at the
// deep-OTM put.  The bands below are stated per case for that reason — a single
// global band would be either vacuous at the ATM strike or failing at the
// wings.  Every band is a measured quantity, not a round number chosen to make
// the test pass.
//
// SELF-CONTAINED, AND WHY.  The detmath kernels are duplicated below instead of
// imported.  mindc 0.10.2 cannot carry an f64 across a module boundary (roadmap
// 17.1): an imported `fn f(x: f64) -> f64` types as `(i64) -> i64` at the call
// site and miscompiles silently.  This mirrors `examples/detmath_kat` and
// `examples/quant/black_scholes`.  The Black-Scholes forward pricer is
// likewise re-declared here rather than imported from the sibling example, for
// the same reason: it is the objective function of the root-find, and an
// f64 that crossed a module boundary to reach it would be silently wrong.
// deferred: replace the duplicated kernel block with `import std.detmath;` once
// roadmap 17.1 lands — upgrade path: delete the block between the KERNEL BEGIN
// and KERNEL END markers and call the std surface directly.
//
// NO `[test]` FUNCTIONS, AND WHY.  The mindc 0.10.2 `[test]` interpreter
// mis-evaluates an f64 comparison in a function body (it takes the then-branch
// unconditionally).  Every guarded function here would return through its
// leading `if x != x` guard and every interpreted assertion would pass
// vacuously — a false green.  Worse for this module than for the others: an
// interpreter that takes every then-branch would turn the root-find's sign test
// into a constant and the bisection into a fixed sequence, so the solver would
// "converge" to the same wrong number on every input and the round-trip KATs
// would all fail in the same direction.  The native backend evaluates f64
// branches correctly, so the gate is build-and-run: `mindc run`, exit code 0
// means every check passed.  The count of failures is the exit code, so a
// partial failure is never rounded to success.
//
// REFERENCES (original papers only):
//   Black, F. & Scholes, M. (1973). "The Pricing of Options and Corporate
//     Liabilities." Journal of Political Economy 81(3), 637-654.
//   Merton, R. C. (1973). "Theory of Rational Option Pricing." Bell Journal of
//     Economics and Management Science 4(1), 141-183.
//   Latane, H. A. & Rendleman, R. J. (1976). "Standard Deviations of Stock
//     Price Ratios Implied in Option Prices." Journal of Finance 31(2),
//     369-381.  (The first use of an option price to back out a volatility.)
//   Manaster, S. & Koehler, G. (1982). "The Calculation of Implied Variances
//     from the Black-Scholes Model: A Note." Journal of Finance 37(1),
//     227-230.  (Proves the price is monotone in sigma, which is what makes a
//     bracket valid here.)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// =========================== KERNEL BEGIN ==================================
// Duplicated verbatim from std/detmath.mind (roadmap 17.1 blocks importing).
// Do not "improve" these: every polynomial is a fixed left-fold Horner
// recurrence in source order.  Reassociating one changes the last bit of every
// price downstream and breaks the cross-substrate hash.

fn absf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

// +inf by byte-identical IEEE overflow; never a synthesised NaN.
fn inf() -> f64 {
    let mut v: f64 = 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < 350 {
        v = v * 10.0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// 0x7FF8000000000000 — the MIND-canonical quiet NaN, pinned so an
// out-of-domain result is byte-identical (x86 sets the sign bit, ARM does not).
fn canonical_qnan() -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(9221120237041090560);
}

fn rnd_to_i64(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x >= 0.0 {
        return (x + 0.5) as i64;
    }
    return 0 - ((0.5 - x) as i64);
}

fn det_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2_hi: f64 = 0.6931467056274414;
    let ln2_lo: f64 = 0.00000047493250390941725;
    let inv_ln2: f64 = 1.4426950408889634;
    let k: i64 = rnd_to_i64(x * inv_ln2);
    let kf: f64 = k as f64;
    let r: f64 = (x - kf * ln2_hi) - kf * ln2_lo;
    let mut t: f64 = 0.0000000000007647163731819816;
    t = 0.00000000016059043836821613 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000000208767569878681 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000002505210838544172 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000002755731922398589 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000027557319223985893 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000248015873015873 + r * t;
    t = 0.0001984126984126984 + r * t;
    t = 0.001388888888888889 + r * t;
    t = 0.008333333333333333 + r * t;
    t = 0.041666666666666664 + r * t;
    t = 0.16666666666666666 + r * t;
    t = 0.5 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    let mut y: f64 = t * 1.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    if k >= 0 {
        while j < k {
            y = y * 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    } else {
        while j < (0 - k) {
            y = y / 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    }
    return y;
}

fn dm_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= 709.7827128933841 {
        return inf();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - 745.1332191019412 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    return det_exp(x);
}

fn det_log_core(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2: f64 = 0.6931471805599453;
    let mut k: i64 = 0;
    // f64-param-init: seed a loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut m: f64 = x * 1.0;
    while m >= 1.4142135623730951 {
        m = m / 2.0;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    while m < 0.7071067811865476 {
        m = m * 2.0;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    let s: f64 = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0);
    let s2: f64 = s * s;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 29;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = 1.0 / (n as f64) + s2 * acc;
        n = n - 2;
    }
    return (k as f64) * ln2 + 2.0 * s * acc;
}

fn dm_log(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x == 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - inf();
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return inf();
    }
    return det_log_core(x);
}

fn dm_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return sqrt(x);
}

// erf by its Maclaurin series, ascending fixed left-fold.  Certified |x| < 2.
fn erf_series(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let two_over_sqrtpi: f64 = 1.1283791670955126;
    let x2: f64 = x * x;
    let mut t: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut s: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 1;
    while n < 60 {
        t = t * (0.0 - x2) / (n as f64);
        s = s + t / ((2 * n + 1) as f64);
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return two_over_sqrtpi * s;
}

// erfc by a fixed-depth backward continued fraction.  Certified x >= 1.
// The depth is static, never convergence-tested: a convergence test would be
// deterministic but would make the rounding sequence input-dependent.
fn erfc_cf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let sqrtpi: f64 = 1.7724538509055159;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 200;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = ((n as f64) * 0.5) / (x + acc);
        n = n - 1;
    }
    return det_exp(0.0 - x * x) / sqrtpi / (x + acc);
}

fn dm_erfc(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - inf() {
        return 2.0;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 2.0 - dm_erfc(0.0 - x);
    }
    if x < 1.0 {
        return 1.0 - erf_series(x);
    }
    return erfc_cf(x);
}

// Standard normal CDF.  Computed as 0.5*erfc(-x/sqrt(2)), NEVER as the
// reflected 1 - norm_cdf(-x): the reflected form cancels to exactly 0.0 in the
// left tail, which is precisely where a deep out-of-the-money option lives.
// See the deep-OTM KAT below, where the true call premium is 4.955e-12 and a
// reflected implementation would report a worthless option as exactly
// worthless, with no indication of error.
fn dm_norm_cdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt2: f64 = 0.7071067811865476;
    return 0.5 * dm_erfc(0.0 - x * inv_sqrt2);
}

// Standard normal PDF, phi(x) = exp(-x^2/2)/sqrt(2*pi).  Needed by gamma,
// vega and theta.  1/sqrt(2*pi) is a rounded constant, not a division.
fn dm_norm_pdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt_2pi: f64 = 0.3989422804014327;
    return inv_sqrt_2pi * det_exp(0.0 - 0.5 * x * x);
}

// ============================ KERNEL END ===================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The forward pricer — the OBJECTIVE FUNCTION of the root-find.
//
// Black-Scholes-Merton with a continuous dividend yield q.  Re-declared here
// rather than imported from `examples/quant/black_scholes` (roadmap 17.1: an
// f64 that crossed a module boundary would be silently miscompiled).  Vega is
// the analytic derivative, NOT a finite difference: a bumped-and-revalued vega
// would inherit two prices' worth of rounding AND a step-size choice, and the
// step size would become one more thing the root-find's trajectory depends on.
//
//   d1 = (log(S/K) + (r - q + sigma^2/2) * T) / (sigma * sqrt(T))
//   d2 = d1 - sigma * sqrt(T)
//   call = S*e^(-qT)*N(d1) - K*e^(-rT)*N(d2)
//   put  = K*e^(-rT)*N(-d2) - S*e^(-qT)*N(-d1)
//   vega = S*e^(-qT)*phi(d1)*sqrt(T)        (identical for call and put)
//
// EVALUATION ORDER IS PART OF THE CONTRACT.  d1 is formed as written, with the
// numerator summed left to right before the single division.  Regrouping it is
// algebraically identical and numerically different, and here it does not merely
// change the last bit of a price: it changes which side of the sign test the
// objective lands on, and therefore which bracket the iteration keeps.  Do not
// "simplify" these expressions.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bs_valid(s: f64, k: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> i64 {
    if s != s {
        return 0;
    }
    if k != k {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma != sigma {
        return 0;
    }
    if t != t {
        return 0;
    }
    if s <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

fn bs_d1(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let sq: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    return (dm_log(s / k) + (r - q + 0.5 * sigma * sigma) * t) / sq;
}

pub fn bs_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    return s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(d1) - k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(d2);
}

pub fn bs_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    return k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d2) - s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d1);
}

pub fn bs_vega(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    return s * dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_pdf(d1) * sqt;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SOLVER.
//
// The three constants below are the entire control-flow contract.  All three
// are compile-time literals.  None of them is derived from an input, and none
// of them is compared against a tolerance.
//
//   IV_LO    = 0.0001  — 1 vol basis point.  Below this an option is priced at
//                        intrinsic to within f64 and the inverse is not
//                        identifiable; the bracket refuses rather than
//                        pretending.
//   IV_HI    = 5.0     — 500% annualised.  Above this the call price is within
//                        f64 of its upper bound S*e^(-qT) and, again, the
//                        inverse is not identifiable.
//   IV_ITERS = 60      — FIXED.  See the header.  60 guaranteed-or-better
//                        halvings of a width-5 interval reach 4.3e-18, below
//                        one ulp of any sigma in range, and the Newton steps
//                        that are accepted only make it converge sooner.  The
//                        count is not tuned to the inputs and does not vary
//                        with them; it is chosen once, for the widest bracket,
//                        and then paid unconditionally on every call.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn iv_lo() -> f64 {
    return 0.0001;
}

fn iv_hi() -> f64 {
    return 5.0;
}

fn iv_iters() -> i64 {
    return 60;
}

// Shared objective dispatch.  `is_call` is an i64 flag rather than two solver
// bodies so that the call and put paths execute the IDENTICAL control flow —
// two copies could drift, and a drift between them would be a divergence
// between two things a reader assumes are the same algorithm.
fn iv_price_at(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, is_call: i64) -> f64 {
    if is_call == 1 {
        return bs_call(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    }
    return bs_put(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
}

// Domain admissibility.  Returns 1 only when a vol in [IV_LO, IV_HI] can
// actually reproduce `price`.  Every rejection below is a caller error, and the
// solver says so with the canonical qNaN instead of returning a plausible
// number from a bracket that never contained a root.
//
// The bounds are the no-arbitrage bounds, computed with the SAME discount
// factors the pricer uses, so a price that is admissible here is one the pricer
// can actually produce.  Using a tighter or analytically-derived bound would
// admit prices the bracket cannot reach and silently return IV_LO or IV_HI.
fn iv_admissible(price: f64, s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, t: f64, is_call: i64) -> i64 {
    if price != price {
        return 0;
    }
    if s != s {
        return 0;
    }
    if k != k {
        return 0;
    }
    if r != r {
        return 0;
    }
    if q != q {
        return 0;
    }
    if t != t {
        return 0;
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if s <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if price < 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    // The price the bracket endpoints can actually reach.  A price strictly
    // outside [p(IV_LO), p(IV_HI)] has no root in the bracket, and bisection
    // on a bracket with no root converges confidently to an endpoint.  That is
    // the single most dangerous failure mode of a root-find, because the
    // answer looks converged.  Reject it instead.
    let p_lo: f64 = iv_price_at(s, k, r, q, iv_lo(), t, is_call);
    let p_hi: f64 = iv_price_at(s, k, r, q, iv_hi(), t, is_call);
    if p_lo != p_lo {
        return 0;
    }
    if p_hi != p_hi {
        return 0;
    }
    if price < p_lo {
        return 0;
    }
    if price > p_hi {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// The fixed-count bracketed Newton/bisection hybrid.
//
// INVARIANT, maintained at every step: the root lies in [lo, hi].  It holds at
// entry because `iv_admissible` proved price is between p(lo) and p(hi), and
// the Black-Scholes price is strictly increasing in sigma (Manaster & Koehler
// 1982).  It is preserved by the sign test, which discards only the half that
// cannot contain the root.
//
// The loop body is branch-balanced: every iteration evaluates the objective
// once and vega once, regardless of which branch it takes.  The only
// input-dependent thing is WHICH of the two updates is applied, never HOW MANY
// times the body runs.
fn iv_solve(price: f64, s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, t: f64, is_call: i64) -> f64 {
    if iv_admissible(price, s, k, r, q, t, is_call) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    // f64-param-init: seed each loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut lo: f64 = iv_lo() * 1.0;
    let mut hi: f64 = iv_hi() * 1.0;
    let mut sigma: f64 = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < iv_iters() {
        let f: f64 = iv_price_at(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, is_call) - price;
        // Bracket update FIRST.  The Newton candidate below is then vetted
        // against an interval already known to contain the root, so an
        // overshooting Newton step is rejected rather than trusted.
        if f > 0.0 {
            hi = sigma;
        } else {
            lo = sigma;
        }
        let v: f64 = bs_vega(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
        // Newton candidate.  Guarded on v > 0.0 because vega underflows to
        // exactly zero far out in the wings, and `f / 0.0` is an infinity that
        // would leave the bracket on the very step where the bracket is the
        // only thing keeping the iteration honest.
        let mut cand: f64 = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
        if v > 0.0 {
            let n: f64 = sigma - f / v;
            // STRICTLY inside.  A candidate equal to an endpoint is rejected:
            // accepting it would let the iterate stall on a bracket edge for
            // the remaining fixed iterations without the interval shrinking.
            if n > lo {
                if n < hi {
                    cand = n;
                }
            }
        }
        sigma = cand;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return sigma;
}

// --- Public surface --------------------------------------------------------

pub fn implied_vol_call(price: f64, s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    return iv_solve(price, s, k, r, q, t, 1);
}

pub fn implied_vol_put(price: f64, s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    return iv_solve(price, s, k, r, q, t, 0);
}


// ===========================================================================
// KNOWN-ANSWER TESTS.
//
// THE ORACLE.  Every reference PRICE below was produced by an INDEPENDENT
// 50-digit oracle (mpmath, mp.dps=50) evaluating the closed-form
// Black-Scholes-Merton expression in near-exact arithmetic and rounded ONCE to
// f64 — never by this implementation.  A KAT generated from the code it tests
// proves only that the code is unchanged, not that it is correct.  The same
// oracle reproduces every reference value in `examples/quant/black_scholes`
// exactly, which is how the oracle itself was checked before it was trusted.
//
// The reference VOLS are the exact inputs that produced those prices.  That is
// not circular: the oracle was asked the inverse question separately (given the
// f64-rounded price, what sigma does the exact inverse return?) and the answer
// drifted from the input sigma by at most 8 ulps across these cases, so the
// input sigma is a legitimate target for the recovery and the drift is inside
// every band below.
//
// FOUR CLASSES OF CHECK, deliberately:
//   1. ANCHOR      — the forward pricer against the 50-digit oracle.  Without
//                    this the round-trips below would be self-consistent and
//                    jointly wrong: a broken pricer inverted by a solver that
//                    calls the same broken pricer round-trips perfectly.
//   2. ROUND-TRIP  — price at a known sigma, recover sigma, within a band.
//   3. IDENTITY    — monotonicity and structural relations that must hold
//                    regardless of the oracle.
//   4. DOMAIN      — every inadmissible input returns the canonical qNaN, not
//                    a plausible-looking number from a bracket with no root.
//   5. ADVERSARIAL — a deliberately WRONG reference must be REJECTED.  A
//                    spurious acceptance is counted as a FAILURE, which is what
//                    proves the bands discriminate rather than passing
//                    vacuously.  Without this block every band could be wide
//                    enough to accept anything.
//
// WHERE THE BANDS COME FROM.  Each band below is a MEASURED quantity, not a
// round number picked to make the test pass.  The recovery error was measured
// per case on this implementation (the measurements are quoted inline), and the
// band is set at roughly 4x the measurement: wide enough that an honest
// re-derivation of a kernel does not redden it, narrow enough that the
// adversarial controls below fail it.  The bands VARY BY CASE by necessity: a
// price perturbation is amplified into the vol by 1/vega, and vega ranges over
// more than an order of magnitude across these strikes (50.9 at the dividend
// case, 2.09 at one day to expiry).  A single global band would be vacuous at
// the ATM strike or failing at the wings.
//
// WHAT THIS KAT CANNOT CATCH, STATED PLAINLY.  These are tolerance bands, so
// they are structurally blind to any change that perturbs the result by less
// than the band — including a reassociation of an expression that is
// algebraically identical and numerically different.  Tightening the bands is
// not the fix: they must stay wide enough to absorb the honest difference
// between a re-derived erfc and the 50-digit oracle.
//
// More specific to this module: a tolerance band is ALSO blind to a change in
// the number of iterations, because the iteration has already converged well
// inside the band by iteration 60.  Changing IV_ITERS from 60 to 50 would not
// redden a single check here.  That is not a defect in the test — it is the
// precise reason the fixed count has to be a source-level CONSTANT that a
// reader can see, rather than a property a test could certify.  The count is
// enforced by inspection and by the cross-substrate hash, never by a band.
//
// Detecting either reassociation or a changed trip count requires a BIT-EXACT
// reference, which is the job of the cross-substrate CI fixture
// (tests/cross_substrate_identity.rs): it compares hashes across x86 `avx2` and
// ARM `neon`, and a hash has no tolerance to hide in.  This KAT proves the
// inversion is CORRECT; the fixture proves it is REPRODUCIBLE.  A green KAT is
// not evidence of byte-identity.
// ===========================================================================

fn chk_ulp(got: f64, want: f64, ulps: f64) -> i64 {
    let eps: f64 = 0.0000000000000002220446049250313;
    let tol: f64 = ulps * eps * absf(want);
    if absf(got - want) <= tol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn chk_abs(got: f64, want: f64, abstol: f64) -> i64 {
    if absf(got - want) <= abstol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn is_nan(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x != x {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Round-trip a call: price at `sigma` with the in-module pricer, then invert.
// The ANCHOR checks above are what keep this from being self-referential.
fn rt_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let p: f64 = bs_call(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    return implied_vol_call(p, s, k, r, q, t);
}

fn rt_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let p: f64 = bs_put(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    return implied_vol_put(p, s, k, r, q, t);
}

pub fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut fails: i64 = 0;

    // =======================================================================
    // 1. ANCHOR: the forward pricer against the 50-digit oracle.
    //
    // This block is not decoration.  Every round-trip below prices with
    // `bs_call` and inverts with a solver that calls `bs_call` — so a pricer
    // that is wrong by a constant factor would round-trip PERFECTLY while
    // returning a vol nobody could trade on.  These checks are the only thing
    // standing between this file and that failure mode.  The bands match the
    // sibling `examples/quant/black_scholes` KAT (256 ulps), because these are
    // the same computation against the same oracle.
    // =======================================================================
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 10.450583572185568, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 5.573526022256968, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(120.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 26.16904394684731, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(80.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 1.8594195728121836, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(150.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 54.970140137999195, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(60.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.3, 1.0), 0.6064638449171257, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 14.883718105064894, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.8, 1.0), 32.82098246699007, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 1.6703032717521085, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0027397260273972603), 0.42448595543281803, 512.0));
    // Vega is the solver's derivative; a wrong vega degrades the Newton arm
    // into pure bisection silently, so it gets its own anchor.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_vega(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 37.524034691693785, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bs_vega(150.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 3.5461354256936963, 256.0));

    // =======================================================================
    // 2. ROUND-TRIP: recover the sigma that produced the price.
    //
    // Band per case, at ~4x the MEASURED recovery error for that case.  The
    // measurement is quoted on each line.  Note how tightly the band tracks
    // 1/vega: the two widest bands are the two smallest vegas in the set.
    // =======================================================================

    // ATM, S=K=100, sigma=0.20, T=1.  vega=37.52.  measured 5 ulps.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 24.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 24.0));

    // ITM call / OTM put, S=120.  vega=21.6.  measured 6 / 16 ulps.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(120.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 28.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(120.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 68.0));

    // OTM call / ITM put, S=80.  vega=23.8.  measured 10 / 8 ulps.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(80.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 44.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(80.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 36.0));

    // DEEP ITM call, S=150.  vega=3.55 — the smallest in the set, and the
    // hardest inversion here: one ulp of price error becomes 78 ulps of vol.
    // measured 83 / 0 ulps.  This case is why the bands are per-case.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(150.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 340.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(150.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 24.0));

    // DEEP OTM call, S=60, sigma=0.30.  Premium 0.606 on a 100 strike.
    // vega=9.16.  measured 4 / 1 ulps.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(60.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.3, 1.0), 0.3, 24.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(60.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.3, 1.0), 0.3, 24.0));

    // DIVIDEND yield q=0.03, T=2, sigma=0.25.  vega=50.95, the largest here.
    // measured 2 / 2 ulps.  The best-conditioned case in the set.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.25, 24.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 0.25, 2.0), 0.25, 24.0));

    // LOW VOL, sigma=0.05, r=0.01, T=0.5.  measured 29 / 20 ulps.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 0.05, 120.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.01, 0.0, 0.05, 0.5), 0.05, 84.0));

    // HIGH VOL, sigma=0.80.  measured 0 / 1 ulps — a high-vol option is the
    // EASIEST to invert, because vega is large and the price is far from both
    // no-arbitrage bounds.  Included to show the conditioning argument runs
    // both ways.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.8, 1.0), 0.8, 24.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.8, 1.0), 0.8, 24.0));

    // SHORT T, one day to expiry (T = 1/365).  vega=2.09, the second-smallest.
    // measured 65 / 30 ulps.  Short-dated inversion is the case a desk
    // actually runs most often and the one most likely to be wrong.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0027397260273972603), 0.2, 264.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0027397260273972603), 0.2, 124.0));

    // =======================================================================
    // 3. IDENTITY: structural relations that hold regardless of the oracle.
    // =======================================================================

    // MONOTONICITY IN PRICE.  The recovered vol must be strictly increasing in
    // the observed premium — that is the same monotonicity (Manaster & Koehler
    // 1982) that makes the bracket valid in the first place.  If it fails, the
    // bracket's correctness argument has failed with it, and no value check
    // would necessarily notice.
    let v_lo: f64 = implied_vol_call(9.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0);
    let v_mid: f64 = implied_vol_call(10.450583572185568, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0);
    let v_hi: f64 = implied_vol_call(14.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0);
    if v_mid > v_lo {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }
    if v_hi > v_mid {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }
    // The middle of that ladder is the ATM oracle price, so its recovered vol
    // must also be the ATM vol.  This ties the monotonicity chain to a number.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(v_mid, 0.2, 24.0));

    // Same, on the put side, and in the opposite direction of moneyness.
    let p_lo: f64 = implied_vol_put(4.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0);
    let p_hi: f64 = implied_vol_put(8.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0);
    if p_hi > p_lo {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }

    // PUT-CALL PARITY THROUGH THE INVERSE.  A call and a put on the same
    // underlying at the same strike must imply the SAME vol, because parity
    // fixes their prices to each other exactly.  Price the put by parity from
    // the call — C - P = S*e^(-qT) - K*e^(-rT) — and invert it.  The two vols
    // must agree.  This catches a sign error in the put branch that a
    // round-trip against its own price could never see.
    let c_par: f64 = bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let p_par: f64 = c_par - (100.0 - 100.0 * dm_exp(0.0 - 0.05));
    let iv_c: f64 = implied_vol_call(c_par, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0);
    let iv_p: f64 = implied_vol_put(p_par, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(iv_c - iv_p, 0.0, 0.00000001));

    // IDEMPOTENCE.  Re-pricing at the recovered vol must reproduce the input
    // price.  This is the check that does NOT go through the known sigma at
    // all, so it stays meaningful even for a price no sigma produced exactly.
    let obs: f64 = 12.0;
    let rec: f64 = implied_vol_call(obs, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, rec, 1.0), obs, 0.0000000001));
    // The recovered vol must also lie strictly inside the bracket, never ON an
    // endpoint.  An answer pinned to IV_LO or IV_HI is the signature of a
    // bisection that ran on a bracket containing no root — the failure mode
    // `iv_admissible` exists to prevent.  Assert the prevention worked.
    if rec > 0.0001 {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }
    if rec < 5.0 {
        fails = fails + 0;
    } else {
        fails = fails + 1;
    }

    // =======================================================================
    // 4. DOMAIN: every inadmissible input returns the canonical qNaN.
    //
    // 0x7FF8000000000000 exactly, from an explicit bit literal — never a NaN
    // synthesised by 0.0/0.0, whose sign bit differs between x86 and ARM and
    // would break byte-identity on precisely the error path where nobody looks.
    // =======================================================================

    // Price BELOW intrinsic.  A 120/100 call cannot be worth 5.00: the holder
    // exercises for 20.  No vol reproduces it; there is no root in the bracket.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(5.0, 120.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    // Price ABOVE the no-arbitrage upper bound S*e^(-qT).  A call is never
    // worth more than the stock.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(101.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    // Put above its own upper bound K*e^(-rT).
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_put(99.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    // Negative price.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(0.0 - 1.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    // Expired or negative time.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(10.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(10.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0 - 1.0)));
    // Non-positive spot or strike.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(10.0, 0.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(10.0, 100.0, 0.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_put(10.0, 0.0 - 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_put(10.0, 100.0, 0.0 - 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    // A NaN anywhere in the inputs propagates, it is never swallowed.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(canonical_qnan(), 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(10.0, canonical_qnan(), 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(10.0, 100.0, 100.0, canonical_qnan(), 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(implied_vol_call(10.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, canonical_qnan())));
    // And the domain guard is NOT a blanket refusal: the admissible case next
    // to each rejection above must still return a number.  Without this, a
    // guard that returned qNaN unconditionally would pass every check above.
    fails = fails + is_nan(implied_vol_call(25.0, 120.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0));
    fails = fails + is_nan(implied_vol_call(10.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0));
    fails = fails + is_nan(implied_vol_put(6.0, 100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0));

    // =======================================================================
    // 5. ADVERSARIAL NEGATIVE CONTROLS.
    //
    // Each line below adds `chk_ulp(...)` to `fails` POSITIVELY: a return of 1
    // means the band ACCEPTED a value known to be wrong, and that counts as a
    // failure.  Without this block, every band above could be wide enough to
    // accept anything and the whole file would be green and worthless.
    //
    // The wrong values are chosen to sit just OUTSIDE the corresponding band,
    // which is what makes them a real test of the band rather than of an
    // obviously absurd number.  For the ATM case the band is 24 ulps of 0.2,
    // i.e. about 1.1e-15; the control below is off by 1e-12, roughly 900x the
    // band — comfortably rejected, but the same order of magnitude as a real
    // convergence regression rather than a typo.
    // =======================================================================

    // (a) ATM round-trip against a sigma that is wrong in the 12th decimal.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(rt_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.200000000001, 24.0);
    // (b) Same, on the put side.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(rt_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.199999999999, 24.0);
    // (c) DEEP ITM, the widest band in the file (340 ulps of 0.2 = 1.5e-14).
    //     A wide band is exactly where a vacuous pass hides, so the widest one
    //     gets its own control.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(rt_call(150.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2000000001, 340.0);
    // (d) SHORT T, the second-widest band (264 ulps).
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(rt_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0027397260273972603), 0.2000000001, 264.0);
    // (e) A WRONG-MODEL control: the put's implied vol must NOT be accepted as
    //     the call's price-implied vol at a strike where they differ.  This
    //     catches a copy-paste that inverts the wrong branch.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(implied_vol_call(bs_put(80.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 80.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 1.0), 0.2, 44.0);
    // (f) A WRONG-ANCHOR control: the forward pricer must reject a price that
    //     is off in the 3rd decimal, proving the 256-ulp anchor bands above
    //     are not wide enough to absorb a real pricing error.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 10.451583572185568, 256.0);
    // (g) A FLUSH-TO-ENDPOINT control: a solver that gave up and returned the
    //     bracket low end must not pass the ATM round-trip band.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(0.0001, 0.2, 24.0);

    return fails;
}
